module gunbc.live_deploy.candidate


data candidate_release_note: String = "THE PER-INVOCATION RELEASE, WHICH THE SLOT SPEC IS NOT. DeploymentSpec answers WHERE a deployment is placed — which instance root, which port, which unit name, which host — and every one of those facts is stable across deploys of blue or of green. CandidateRelease answers WHAT is being installed on this invocation, and every one of ITS facts changes on every deploy. Fusing them would put a mutable revision inside the static slot topology, which is why deployment_spec_srv1 takes the revision as a parameter rather than owning it: the slot identity stays a constant of the fleet model and the release travels beside it.\\n\\nTWO MEMBERS BECAUSE THE PROCESS REPORTS TWO. RunningReleaseObservation carries revision and surface_bundle_identity as separate wire members precisely because they fail independently, and a candidate that carried only one of them could not be compared against the half it omitted. So the candidate is the EXPECTATION and the observation is the READING, member for member, and readiness is the equality between them."

data candidate_observed_once_note: String = "OBSERVED ONCE, AT THE PUBLIC LAUNCHER, BEFORE ANY HOST MUTATION — and the placement is the whole property. A revision rediscovered deeper in the chain (in the emitter, in readiness, in a retry arm) is a SECOND observation of a tree that the deploy itself may have changed underneath, so the unit could be emitted with one revision and verified against another, and every comparison downstream would still look internally consistent. Observing once means the launcher's reading is the only revision in the invocation and every later use is a reference to that one value, which is what makes 'the checkout's sha, the emitted launch sha, and the process-reported sha are equal' a claim with a single subject rather than three readings that happen to agree.\\n\\nIT IS THE DEPLOYING CHECKOUT, NOT A SLOT'S DISK HEAD. The distinction is the one the operator ruling turns on. This observation runs in the CI workspace that is performing the deploy, and answers 'which candidate am I installing' — a fact the deploying job is the only party that knows. Reading a git HEAD inside a slot tree ON THE HOST would answer a different question ('what does that directory contain'), and using it as the ACTIVE predecessor is exactly the disk-derived ordering the ruling struck down, because the disk diverges from the running process whenever a sync is interrupted or lands out of order. Observing the candidate here is safe for the same reason that one is not: nothing has been mutated yet, and the checkout is the deploying job's own workspace.\\n\\nTHE SURFACE HALF NEEDS NO OBSERVATION. expected_surface_identity is a pure function of this tree, so it is computed rather than read.\\n\\nTHE SURFACE HALF IS CONSUMED, NOT MERELY PUBLISHED. apply.dag carries this whole value — both members — into the readiness poll, and readiness compares the process-reported surface_bundle_identity against expected_surface_identity rather than re-materializing the bundle at comparison time. That distinction is the point: a recomputed expectation would describe the tree AS IT IS WHEN COMPARED, so if the deploying checkout changed after admission the expectation and the verdict would drift together and agree with a process other than the one admitted. An earlier revision of this note said the member was captured and dropped; that was true when written and is false now."

type CandidateRelease {
  revision: std.types.CommitSha
  expected_surface_identity: std.content_hash.Fnv1a64Structural
}

// A REVISION NAMES A COMMIT; THE DEPLOY SHIPS A WORKING TREE. Those are the same bytes only when the
// checkout is clean, and nothing above established that. The sync's working-tree leg rsyncs the runner
// checkout wholesale except gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_scope deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes, so an edit
// or an untracked file is DEPLOYED while HEAD does not name it — and the resulting candidate
// { revision: A, surface: B } is self-consistent all the way down: the emitter writes A into ExecStart, the
// process reports A, readiness compares A against A and greens, and the ancestry decision downstream
// orders the deployment as A. Nothing in the chain can see that the bytes were never A's, because every
// party is reading the same wrong answer. This is the one class the identity channel cannot catch by
// construction, which is why it is refused at admission instead.
//
// THE UNTRACKED ARM IS NOT A COURTESY. `--delete` on the working-tree leg means a stray file in the
// checkout is copied to the host and then persists there; the deployment's own source sync is the transport
// that makes an untracked path load-bearing. It is scoped to DEPLOYED paths for the same reason: a file
// under target/ or .git/ is excluded by the sync, so refusing on it would be refusing on bytes that never
// leave the runner — a fail-closed arm that fires on a state which is not the invalid one is just noise,
// and noise is what gets suppressed later.
//
// FOUR SEPARATE PATH LISTS, NOT A COUNT OR A BOOL. Staged, unstaged-tracked, untracked-deployed and
// ignored-deployed have different remedies — commit the index, commit or discard the edit, add or clean the
// file, and for the last one either widen the deployed-scope exclusions or remove the file — and a deploy
// operator reading the refusal needs to know which, on which path. A `dirty: Bool` would be the same refusal
// with the locating information deleted.
//
// ignored_deployed_paths is its OWN list rather than being merged into untracked_deployed_paths, because the
// two answer to different authorities: an untracked file is one Git does not track yet, an ignored file is one
// the repository has deliberately told Git to stop reporting. Folding them would tell an operator to `git add`
// a path that .gitignore exists to keep out of the index.
//
// It closes a hole the first version of this admission had. `git status --porcelain=v1 -z
// --untracked-files=all` does not report ignored files at all, while the source sync excludes only the two
// prefixes deployed_tree_scope names — so an ignored path outside those two (this repository ignores .env,
// /.gunbc/, .claude/, /target-codex/, /.cargo-home/, and, inside a source root the graph actually reads,
// src/v2/test/claim/manual/sg2_scratch_probe.dag) was copied by rsync while the admission reported the tree
// clean. The resulting state is self-consistent and therefore invisible to every later comparison: HEAD names
// revision A, rsync installs bytes B, the candidate's expected surface is rendered from B, the process reports
// A and B, and readiness compares A against A and B against B and greens. That is exactly the false identity
// this admission exists to make unrepresentable, surviving inside it.
type CandidateCheckoutObservation
  = CandidateCheckoutClean
  | CandidateCheckoutDirty {
      tracked_changes: List<String>,
      staged_changes: List<String>,
      untracked_deployed_paths: List<String>,
      ignored_deployed_paths: List<String>,
    }
  | CandidateCheckoutUnobservable { detail: String }

type CandidateUnobservableCause
  = CandidateHeadCommitRefused
  | CandidateRevisionEmpty
  | CandidateRevisionMalformed { text: String }
  | CandidateCheckoutNotClean {
      tracked_changes: List<String>,
      staged_changes: List<String>,
      untracked_deployed_paths: List<String>,
      ignored_deployed_paths: List<String>,
    }
  | CandidateCheckoutStatusRefused { detail: String }

type CandidateObservation
  = CandidateObserved { candidate: CandidateRelease }
  | CandidateUnobservable { cause: CandidateUnobservableCause }

data candidate_unobservable_causes_note: String = "THREE CAUSES, ALL REFUSALS, NONE OF THEM A DEFAULT. A deploy that cannot name the candidate it is installing has nothing to emit into ExecStart and nothing to verify the routed process against, so there is no arm here that proceeds with a placeholder, an abbreviated sha, or the string that happened to arrive. Refused means the probe itself did not answer (not a repository, git unavailable); Empty means it answered with nothing, which a bare success flag would have read as success; Malformed means it answered with something that is not a 40-digit lowercase object name — a tag, a branch, an abbreviated sha — which is the case a length-free check accepts and an ancestry probe then silently mis-answers.\\n\\nEmpty is kept distinct from Malformed rather than folded into it because their remedies differ: an empty stdout means the observation seam is broken, while a non-sha text means the seam works and the repository is in a state (detached, unborn, or a ref that does not resolve) the deploy must not paper over."

// The scan carries `expect_rename_source` because porcelain v1 with -z emits a rename or copy as TWO
// NUL-separated fields — the record, then the source path — and the source path is a bare filename. There is
// no shape test that separates it from a record: "ab cdef" is a legal path and would pass any
// "third character is a space" check. Only the previous record's status letter says a continuation follows,
// so the parse is stateful by necessity rather than by preference. Reading it shape-first would attribute a
// rename's source path to a phantom status and, worse, do so only for paths that happen to look like records.
//
// `malformed` refuses rather than skipping. A field that is neither a continuation nor a well-formed record
// means this is not the format we think it is, and silently dropping it would turn an unreadable status into
// a clean one — the empty-observation narrow DESIGN §5 names, where ⊥-as-ignorance is rendered as
// ⊥-as-answer and the deploy proceeds because nothing was found.
type CheckoutStatusScan {
  staged: List<String>
  tracked: List<String>
  untracked: List<String>
  expect_rename_source: Bool
  malformed: Bool
}

fn scan_checkout_status_field(acc: CheckoutStatusScan, field: String) -> CheckoutStatusScan {
  if field == "" {
    acc
  } else if acc.expect_rename_source {
    CheckoutStatusScan {
      staged: acc.staged,
      tracked: acc.tracked,
      untracked: acc.untracked,
      expect_rename_source: false,
      malformed: acc.malformed,
    }
  } else if string_length(s: field) < 4 || substring(s: field, start: 2, end: 3) != " " {
    CheckoutStatusScan {
      staged: acc.staged,
      tracked: acc.tracked,
      untracked: acc.untracked,
      expect_rename_source: false,
      malformed: true,
    }
  } else {
    let x = substring(s: field, start: 0, end: 1)
    let y = substring(s: field, start: 1, end: 2)
    let path = substring(s: field, start: 3, end: string_length(s: field))
    if x == "?" && y == "?" {
      CheckoutStatusScan {
        staged: acc.staged,
        tracked: acc.tracked,
        untracked: if path_is_deployed(path: path) {
          v2.std.algebra.list_append(acc.untracked, [path])
        } else {
          acc.untracked
        },
        expect_rename_source: false,
        malformed: acc.malformed,
      }
    } else {
      CheckoutStatusScan {
        staged: if x != " " { v2.std.algebra.list_append(acc.staged, [path]) } else { acc.staged },
        tracked: if y != " " { v2.std.algebra.list_append(acc.tracked, [path]) } else { acc.tracked },
        untracked: acc.untracked,
        expect_rename_source: x == "R" || x == "C",
        malformed: acc.malformed,
      }
    }
  }
}

fn scan_checkout_status(entries_nul: String) -> CheckoutStatusScan {
  fold(
    split(s: entries_nul, delimiter: from_code_point(0)),
    init: CheckoutStatusScan {
      staged: [],
      tracked: [],
      untracked: [],
      expect_rename_source: false,
      malformed: false,
    },
    f: (acc, field) => scan_checkout_status_field(acc: acc, field: field),
  )
}

fn scan_ignored_deployed_paths(paths_nul: String) -> List<String> {
  fold(
    split(s: paths_nul, delimiter: from_code_point(0)),
    init: [],
    f: (acc, path) =>
      if path != "" && path_is_deployed(path: path) { v2.std.algebra.list_append(acc, [path]) } else { acc },
  )
}

fn observe_candidate_checkout() -> CandidateCheckoutObservation {
  let status = git.Inspect.WorkingTreeStatus(expect: ExpectSuccess)
  let ignored = git.Inspect.IgnoredFiles(expect: ExpectSuccess)
  if !status.success {
    CandidateCheckoutUnobservable {
      detail: join(["git status did not answer for the deploying checkout: ", status.stderr], ""),
    }
  } else if !ignored.success {
    CandidateCheckoutUnobservable {
      detail: join(["git ls-files --others --ignored did not answer for the deploying checkout: ", ignored.stderr], ""),
    }
  } else {
    let scan = scan_checkout_status(entries_nul: status.entries_nul)
    let ignored_deployed = scan_ignored_deployed_paths(paths_nul: ignored.paths_nul)
    if scan.malformed {
      CandidateCheckoutUnobservable {
        detail: "git status --porcelain=v1 -z produced a field that is neither a well-formed record nor a rename source; the working tree state is not readable and is not assumed clean",
      }
    } else if scan.expect_rename_source {
      CandidateCheckoutUnobservable {
        detail: "git status --porcelain=v1 -z ended on a rename or copy record with no source field; the entry list is truncated and is not assumed clean",
      }
    } else if length(scan.staged) == 0
      && length(scan.tracked) == 0
      && length(scan.untracked) == 0
      && length(ignored_deployed) == 0 {
      CandidateCheckoutClean
    } else {
      CandidateCheckoutDirty {
        tracked_changes: scan.tracked,
        staged_changes: scan.staged,
        untracked_deployed_paths: scan.untracked,
        ignored_deployed_paths: ignored_deployed,
      }
    }
  }
}

fn observe_candidate_release() -> CandidateObservation {
  let head = git.Inspect.HeadCommit(expect: ExpectSuccess)
  if !head.success {
    CandidateUnobservable { cause: CandidateHeadCommitRefused }
  } else if (head.sha as String) == "" {
    CandidateUnobservable { cause: CandidateRevisionEmpty }
  } else if !release_revision_text_valid(text: head.sha as String) {
    CandidateUnobservable { cause: CandidateRevisionMalformed { text: head.sha as String } }
  } else {
    match observe_candidate_checkout() {
      CandidateCheckoutUnobservable { detail: d } =>
        CandidateUnobservable { cause: CandidateCheckoutStatusRefused { detail: d } }
      CandidateCheckoutDirty {
        tracked_changes: t,
        staged_changes: s,
        untracked_deployed_paths: u,
        ignored_deployed_paths: i,
      } =>
        CandidateUnobservable {
          cause: CandidateCheckoutNotClean {
            tracked_changes: t,
            staged_changes: s,
            untracked_deployed_paths: u,
            ignored_deployed_paths: i,
          }
        }
      CandidateCheckoutClean =>
        CandidateObserved {
          candidate: CandidateRelease {
            revision: head.sha,
            expected_surface_identity: materialize_live_roadmap_site_surface_bundle().bundle_identity,
          },
        }
    }
  }
}

// The cleanliness read comes AFTER the revision read and BEFORE the surface is rendered, and the order is
// the point. The surface identity is a fingerprint of the working tree; computing it first and then
// discovering the tree is dirty would mean the refusal was constructed from bytes we had already decided not
// to trust. Reading the status first means a dirty tree never reaches the renderer at all — nothing derived
// from untrusted bytes is built, so there is no intermediate value a later arm could mistakenly use.

data observe_candidate_release_is_a_function_note: String = "A FUNCTION AND NOT A TOP-LEVEL data, FOR THE REASON gunbc.stage0_rust_host_observation ALREADY PAID FOR. A top-level data declaration is evaluated by IMPORTING it, so a module that merely named this symbol in an import list would perform Git I/O as a consequence of loading — and that is not hypothetical: a dead import of exactly that shape reached git.Inspect.HeadCommit under the hermetic envelope and took down a 105-row discovery batch. As a function the effect is reachable only by CALLING it, so every witness in this lane may import CandidateRelease, the cause coproduct, and the comparison predicates without observing any host."

fn candidate_unobservable_detail(cause: CandidateUnobservableCause) -> String {
  match cause {
    CandidateHeadCommitRefused =>
      "CandidateHeadCommitRefused: git.Inspect.HeadCommit did not answer for the deploying checkout"
    CandidateRevisionEmpty =>
      "CandidateRevisionEmpty: git.Inspect.HeadCommit succeeded with an empty revision"
    CandidateRevisionMalformed { text: t } =>
      join(["CandidateRevisionMalformed: the deploying checkout reported '", t, "', which is not a 40-digit lowercase object name"], "")
    CandidateCheckoutStatusRefused { detail: d } =>
      join(["CandidateCheckoutStatusRefused: ", d], "")
    CandidateCheckoutNotClean {
      tracked_changes: t,
      staged_changes: s,
      untracked_deployed_paths: u,
      ignored_deployed_paths: i,
    } =>
      join([
        "CandidateCheckoutNotClean: the deploying checkout's working tree does not equal HEAD, and the",
        " source sync ships the working tree — so this deploy would install bytes the revision does not",
        " name and every later comparison would agree with itself. staged=", render_candidate_paths(paths: s),
        " tracked=", render_candidate_paths(paths: t),
        " untracked-deployed=", render_candidate_paths(paths: u),
        " ignored-deployed=", render_candidate_paths(paths: i),
      ], "")
  }
}

fn render_candidate_paths(paths: List<String>) -> String {
  if length(paths) == 0 { "(none)" } else { join(paths, ", ") }
}

fn candidate_release_matches_identity(
  candidate: CandidateRelease,
  identity: gunbc.running_release_identity.RunningReleaseIdentity,
) -> Bool {
  running_release_revision_equals(identity: identity, expected: candidate.revision)
    && running_release_surface_equals(identity: identity, expected: candidate.expected_surface_identity)
}

data candidate_release_matches_identity_note: String = "THE CONJUNCTION EXISTS AS A CONVENIENCE FOR CALLERS THAT NEED BOTH, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE ONLY SURFACE. running_release_revision_equals and running_release_surface_equals stay separately callable because a deploy must be able to say WHICH half failed — right revision with a stale surface means the graph did not rebind, right surface at an unexpected revision means another candidate won the route, and the two have different remedies. Consumers that must locate the failure call the halves and construct a typed cause; only a consumer that genuinely needs the single 'is this the release I admitted' bit calls this."
